/*! \page architecture_map_howto Maintaining the Architecture Map

\brief How the \ref architecture_map diagram is generated, and how to keep it current when
components are added, renamed, or moved.

\section amh_how How it is built

The map is a single Graphviz digraph rendered by Doxygen. Three pieces cooperate:

- \c docs/architecture.dot -- the graph. Each component is a node; the order / execution-
  report data path is drawn with solid edges and the control plane (HA election, credential
  CRUD, WAL fanout) with dashed edges.
- \c docs/architecture_map.dox -- a page that embeds the graph with the \c \\dotfile
  command. This is what makes Doxygen render the PNG and build the clickable image map.
- The \c Doxyfile -- \c HAVE_DOT=YES lets Doxygen invoke Graphviz \c dot, and
  \c DOTFILE_DIRS=docs tells \c \\dotfile where to find \c architecture.dot.

\section amh_links What makes the boxes clickable

Each node carries a \c URL attribute of this form:

\verbatim
matching_engine [label="Matching Engine",
                 URL="\ref matching_engine"];
\endverbatim

Graphviz stores the string verbatim, but Doxygen rewrites any \c \\ref inside a node \c URL
into a real link to that page before it emits the HTML image map. So the box ends up
pointing at the component's own documentation page -- with no hand-written HTML and no
hard-coded output paths.

\section amh_labels The page labels

Each component doc declares an \e explicit page label on its top-level heading, using
Doxygen's \c {\#label} syntax. The label is simply the markdown file's basename without the
\c .md extension:

\verbatim
# Matching Engine {#matching_engine}
\endverbatim

So \c docs/applications/fix_order_gateway.md carries \c {\#fix_order_gateway} and
\c docs/design/wal_and_ha.md carries \c {\#wal_and_ha}; the map then refers to those with a
plain \c "\\ref fix_order_gateway".

\par Why explicit labels and not Doxygen's auto-generated names
Older Doxygen releases -- notably 1.8.14, which is the newest packaged for our RHEL8 build
hosts -- mangle a markdown file's \e auto label differently from the 1.9.x line used on
dev machines (the \c md_docs_2applications_2... form). A map built against an auto label
therefore resolves on one machine and silently breaks on the other: the unresolved \c \\ref
collapses to \c href="../../" (a bare directory), which a browser opens as a directory
listing -- or, on Windows, a file-chooser dialog -- instead of the doc. An explicit
\c {\#label} is identical on every Doxygen version, so the map is portable across them.

\section amh_add Adding a new component

1. Write the component's documentation as usual, e.g. \c docs/applications/foo_service.md,
   and give its top-level heading an explicit label matching the file's basename:
\verbatim
# Foo Service {#foo_service}
\endverbatim
2. Add a node to \c docs/architecture.dot whose \c URL is a plain \c \\ref to that label:
\verbatim
foo_service [label="Foo Service\n(what it does)",
             URL="\ref foo_service"];
\endverbatim
3. Add the edges to and from it. Use \c style=dashed for anything that is \e not on the
   order / execution-report data path.
4. Rebuild the docs: \c doxygen \c Doxyfile, or the \c doxygen_docs CMake target.

\section amh_safety Why a broken link cannot slip through

Two layers guard against a dead box:

- \b WARN_AS_ERROR. The build sets \c WARN_AS_ERROR=FAIL_ON_WARNINGS, so a \c \\ref that
  names a missing page -- a typo, or a doc that was renamed or moved -- fails the build on
  recent Doxygen. Rename a component doc and the map's \c URL fails the build until you
  update it, which is exactly the reminder you want.
- \b A \b post-build \b check. Doxygen 1.8.14 (our RHEL8 hosts) does \e not fail the build
  on an unresolved \c \\ref inside a node \c URL; it silently emits \c href="../../". So the
  docs build also runs \c scripts/check_architecture_map.py, which greps the generated image
  map for \c href="../../" and fails if any box points at a bare directory. This catches the
  one case \c WARN_AS_ERROR misses on the older toolchain.

\section amh_see See also

- \ref architecture_map -- the diagram this page describes.
- \ref architecture "Architecture" -- the narrative topology, port allocation, and
  order flow.

*/
